#lang saffron
;; saffron-lang-plan open question 3, answered: a Saffron file needs NO type
;; annotation anywhere, `main` included.
;;
;; `(defn main [] : int ...)` is awkward in a file whose whole point is having
;; no annotations -- but until now it was mandatory.  An unannotated Saffron
;; function defaults to `: any` (D3), and `main` is the one place that is wrong:
;; the emitted C got `return TUR_TAG(...)` inside a function declared `int` --
;;
;;   error: incompatible types when returning type 'tur_tagged_t' but 'int'
;;          was expected
;;
;; a cc error with no Turmeric diagnostic in front of it.  Every other Saffron
;; fixture writes `: int` on main, which is exactly the annotation the dialect
;; exists to remove; this one does not, and that is what it pins.
;;
;; `main` defaults to `:int` rather than the plan's proposed `:nil`, so its
;; meaning does not fork per `#lang`: an explicit exit code still works, and a
;; body yielding something else is an ordinary return-type error against `int`
;; -- pointing at the body, not at cc.  Only the ZERO-arity `main`.
;;
;; Note what is NOT annotated below: `describe` takes an `any` and returns an
;; `any`, `main` returns `int` by the rule above, and the vector is a
;; `(Vec any)` from the S6 literal widen.

(defn describe [x]
  (type-of x))

(defn main []
  (let [v [1 "two" 7.1]]
    (println (vec-len v))
    (println (describe (vec-get v 0)))
    (println (describe (vec-get v 1)))
    (println (describe (vec-get v 2)))
    (vec-free v))
  0)
